Here’s a single‑file HTML page that does exactly what you asked for: a button that, when clicked, shows a random joke from a list of four jokes.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
  body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    margin: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
  #joke {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: #333;
    min-height: 2rem;   /* keep space even when empty */
  }
  button {
    padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Click the button for a joke!</h1>
<button id="jokeBtn">Tell me a joke</button>

<div id="joke"></div>

<script>
  // Array of jokes
  const jokes = [
    "Why don’t scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
    "I told my wife she was drawing her eyebrows too high. She looked surprised.",
    "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
    "I’m reading a book on anti‑gravity. It’s impossible to put down!"
  ];

  // Grab the button and the joke display element
  const btn = document.getElementById('jokeBtn');
  const jokeDiv = document.getElementById('joke');

  // Function to pick a random joke and display it
  function showRandomJoke() {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
    jokeDiv.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
  }

  // Attach click handler
  btn.addEventListener('click', showRandomJoke);
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
1. **HTML** – A button (`#jokeBtn`) and a `<div>` (`#joke`) to display the joke.
2. **CSS** – Very light styling to center the content and make the button look nice.
3. **JavaScript** –  
   * An array `jokes` holds the four jokes.  
   * `showRandomJoke()` picks a random index and sets the text of the joke `<div>`.  
   * The button’s `click` event triggers this function.

Save the code as `index.html` and open it in any browser. Each click will show a new random joke. Enjoy!

**Solution Overview**

The task is to print all elements that appear in *both* arrays, **without allocating any extra data structure** (no hash‑set, no auxiliary list, etc.).  
The simplest way to achieve this in *O(1)* extra space is:

1. **Sort** both arrays in place.  
   Sorting uses only the array’s own memory (the standard `std::sort` implementation is in‑place).

2. Use two indices (`i` for the first array, `j` for the second) and walk through the arrays simultaneously – the classic “two‑pointer” technique.

   * If `a[i] == b[j]` → the value is common → print it and advance both pointers.  
   * If `a[i] < b[j]` → advance `i` (the smaller value cannot match any later element of `b`).  
   * Otherwise (`a[i] > b[j]`) → advance `j`.

This algorithm automatically handles duplicates: every time a value appears in both arrays, it will be printed as many times as it appears in the *intersection*.

The only extra memory used is a few integer variables (`i`, `j`, `n`, `m`), so the space complexity is **O(1)**.

