## C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
   Prints the common elements of two arrays.
   The arrays are sorted in place; no extra containers are used.
*/
void printCommonElements(vector<int> &a, vector<int> &b) {
    // Sort both arrays in place
    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    sort(b.begin(), b.end());

    size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    bool found = false;

    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a[i] == b[j]) {
            cout << a[i] << ' ';
            ++i; ++j;
            found = true;
        } else if (a[i] < b[j]) {
            ++i;
        } else {            // a[i] > b[j]
            ++j;
        }
    }

    if (!found) {
        cout << "No common elements";
    }
    cout << '\n';
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;          // no input
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> a[i];

    cin >> m;
    vector<int> b(m);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) cin >> b[i];

    printCommonElements(a, b);
    return 0;
}
```

### How to Run

Compile with a C++17 compiler:

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -pipe -static -s -o common common.cpp
```

Run and provide input in the following format:

```
<length of first array>
<elements of first array>
<length of second array>
<elements of second array>
```

**Example**

```
Input:
6
1 2 2 3 4 5
5
2 2 4 6 7

Output:
2 2 4 
```

---

## Complexity Analysis

| Step | Time | Extra Space |
|------|------|-------------|
| Sorting `a` | `O(n log n)` | `O(1)` |
| Sorting `b` | `O(m log m)` | `O(1)` |
| Two‑pointer scan | `O(n + m)` | `O(1)` |
| **Total** | **`O(n log n + m log m)`** | **`O(1)`** |

---

## Why No Extra Data Structures?

- We only use the input arrays themselves (they are sorted in place).
- The algorithm never allocates another array, vector, set, map, or any container to hold intermediate results.
- The only auxiliary variables are simple integers (`i`, `j`, `found`), which satisfy the “no extra data structures” requirement.

Feel free to adapt the code to other languages (Java, Python, etc.) – the same two‑pointer logic applies.

